{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/343884-ready-action-aiming-a-gun","id":343884,"title":"Ready Action + Aiming a Gun.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-12-18T07:24:12-08:00","id":343884,"title":"Ready Action + Aiming a Gun.","content":"Okay, so disclaimer: My question is more mechanics than anything, and I -assumed- that werewolf-players might more likely know this answer, by sheer dint of how often you get into conflict with stuff. So: don't 'teach' me which splat does or doesn't. Frankly my dears? I don't give a damn.<br /> <br /> Scenario: I am playing a PC (We'll say a Irraka Iron Master so the flavor matches the avenue! Yay!) who is in a tense situation with his pack, and another. The Alpha's are negotiating, but guns have been drawn all-around; making this a pretty-tense scene. (for some: again, I don't care about the fact that some werewolves could shrug guns off.) I have chosen to take 3 consecutive actions/turns to aim at a guy from the other pack. (Story-wise: because my dude's a pro, and Mechanics-wise: to get that +3 aiming-bonus)<br /> <br /> Question: I want that aiming bonus, but I also want to make sure with my ST that it's kosher to ALSO say: &quot;If the guy in front of me sneezes, or looks at me funny in a way I don't like: I cap him.&quot; as a ready-action. Now...which way does mechanics run?<br /> <br /> 1. I -must- declare each turn that I am Aiming, and that's it: I aim, and if the dude moves: oh well, because my action was declared that turn as aiming. (until we reach turn 4, where I've aimed, and am ready to fire, or that Ready Action described earlier.)<br /> <br /> 2. I declare I'm aiming all three turns, but let my ST know that I am -also- readying an action to fire at the guy: &quot;I'm trying to get my aim nice, but if he moves before I get past turn 3, I will interrupt my own aiming-action to fire upon him with whatever 'good-aim' I have already.&quot; Do I lose my own aiming actions if the Ready Action triggers, or do I retain what bonus I've accumulated so far?<br /> <br /> 3. I must either declare one or the other. Even if I've aimed and lined up the shot by turn 3, I lose my aiming-bonus if on turn 4, I declare that I'm Readying an Action to fire upon him, should he move in a way I dislike.<br /> <br /> Thank you in advance for your help.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1219-shake","name":"Shake","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1219_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"20"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-18T09:05:08-08:00","id":343925,"title":"","content":"This comes up alot in my games and I've never liked dealing with it.<br /> <br /> If you've been Aiming than you get the bonus dice unless the situation changes. In your situation I say if combat breaks out everyone has to roll initiative normally: it's a stand-off, no one has more preparation than anyone else. Basically EVERYONE is holding an action and there needs to be a random way of determining who's held action goes off first or at least in what order.<br /> <br /> Alternatively, and more entertainingly, one could allow all of the held actions from each individual present to go off at once and then roll normal initiative. That's be nuts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/785-full-time-gm","name":"Full Time GM","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar785_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"174"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}